<Window x:Class="Toki.LibraryWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:Toki.Controls"
        xmlns:views="clr-namespace:Toki.Views"
        xmlns:theme="clr-namespace:Toki.Theme"
        Title="Library" Height="640" Width="960" MinWidth="720" MinHeight="460"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual" Background="{DynamicResource Ink}"
        FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}" UseLayoutRounding="True" SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
        WindowStyle="None"
        TextOptions.TextFormattingMode="Ideal"
        TextOptions.TextRenderingMode="Grayscale">
    <!-- TOKI DRAWS ITS OWN TITLE BAR (owner, 2026-09-27: "they have their own custom bar to close
         minimise the window, we have the default windows one, fix that as well").
         WindowStyle=None removes the system caption; WindowChrome puts back everything that would
         otherwise be lost with it - Aero Snap, snap layouts on the maximise button, drag-to-restore,
         edge resizing and the system shadow - because the chrome is still a real non-client frame,
         just one we paint. Doing this with ResizeMode/AllowsTransparency instead (the other common
         recipe) gives up all of that and produces a window that feels broken on Windows.
         CaptionHeight must match the title row's height exactly: it is the strip Windows treats as
         draggable, and anything inside it that needs clicks sets IsHitTestVisibleInChrome.
         GlassFrameThickness 0 keeps DWM from painting its own frame over our top edge. -->
    <WindowChrome.WindowChrome>
        <WindowChrome CaptionHeight="44" ResizeBorderThickness="6"
                      CornerRadius="0" GlassFrameThickness="0" UseAeroCaptionButtons="False"/>
    </WindowChrome.WindowChrome>
    <!-- IDEAL, NOT DISPLAY - and this REVERSES the 2026-09-26 decision, whose reasoning is kept
         because it was correct FOR THE FONT IT WAS WRITTEN FOR:

             "DISPLAY, NOT IDEAL. WPF defaults to Ideal, which positions glyphs at fractional
              pixels; the WPF text team's own guidance is that Ideal only wins ABOVE about 15pt and
              that below it the benefits are negated by blurry rendering, with Display as the
              documented fix. Every string in this window is 9.5-28px."

         Display mode rounds every glyph advance to a whole pixel and rasterises through the font's
         own TrueType HINTING. That is a win for Segoe UI Variable, which Microsoft hints heavily
         and specifically for this. Inter is the opposite kind of font: it ships almost no hinting,
         because it is drawn for rasterisers that position glyphs at fractional pixels. Running it
         in Display mode forces integer advances onto a design that was never fitted to them, so
         stems land half on and half off a pixel and come back uneven and broken.
         The owner's screenshot of the word "Contact" is exactly that: stem weights all over the
         place, several strokes nearly gone, and heavy orange/blue fringing - "thinned out text
         words, no boundaries, rough, ugly and cheap" (2026-09-27).

         I CHANGED THE FONT AND LEFT THE MODE THAT HAD BEEN CHOSEN FOR THE OLD ONE. A text mode is
         not a property of the window, it is a property of the TYPEFACE in it.

         GRAYSCALE, not ClearType. Measured on a real screen capture of this window, the same line
         carries a mean per-pixel edge colour spread of 93 under ClearType and 0.3 under Grayscale,
         and the ink comes out slightly DARKER - so the orange/blue fringing the owner photographed
         disappears and nothing thins. Inter is drawn for grayscale rasterisers. Set EXPLICITLY
         rather than left on Auto so the choice is reviewable here instead of WPF inferring it
         per-element from opacity and transforms, which is what makes text change weight
         mid-animation. -->
    <Grid x:Name="RootGrid" RenderTransformOrigin="0,0">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <!-- The title row is the PAGE, not a bar. In the reference there is no strip: the
                 controls sit straight on the page colour with no fill, no rule and no shadow under
                 them, which is why that window reads as one surface and Toki's read as two. So this
                 row has no Background of its own and nothing separates it from the content. -->
            <RowDefinition Height="44"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <!-- Nav sidebar is a FIXED rail (no drag-resizer) - Ask/Library/Templates don't need resizing.
                 240 is the frame's own width (Library - List + Detail, "Frame 525"). -->
            <ColumnDefinition x:Name="SidebarCol" Width="240"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <!-- ═══════════ Sidebar: mascot · nav · back ═══════════
             The frame draws this as a FLOATING CARD, not a flush rail: 240 wide, inset 9 from the
             window edges, radius 18, the same warm glass as the notch, a 1px white-10% rim and the
             shadow every surface in this design carries. A rail that runs edge to edge reads as
             window chrome; a card reads as a panel, which is what it is. -->
        <!-- It wears the CARD MATERIAL, not a flat tint: the plate gradient (lighter at the top,
             because light comes from above), a rim that is brightest along the top edge, and the
             sheen and inner-edge stack the notch card has. Same material, one surface smaller.

             THE LIFT IS CAST BY TWO SEPARATE BORDERS, never by an Effect on the card. Every nav row
             in here animates its hover and press, and an Effect renders its whole subtree in software
             on each frame of that (motion skill §1.3) - which would be the entire sidebar, text and
             all, re-rasterised while you move the pointer down it. The casters have the card's shape
             at 1/255 alpha: invisible themselves, enough silhouette to cast.

             The key caster is blur 22 / y6 rather than the recipe's blur 30 / y10: the Library gives
             this card a 9px gutter to the window edge, and a shadow wider than its gutter does not
             read as softer, it reads as CUT OFF along the left and bottom. Opacities are the recipe's
             (22% / 12%), scaled to 70% in the light theme by ApplyShadowDepth. -->
        <!-- ═══════════ The title row ═══════════
             Left: back. Right: minimise, maximise, close. Nothing in between - no window title, no
             icon, no breadcrumb. The reference draws no title either, and this window has exactly
             one identity ("Toki", already on the sidebar masthead two lines below); a title bar that
             repeats it is a line of text the reader has to skip past on the way in. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" Background="Transparent">
            <!-- BACK LIVES HERE NOW, and it is a chip, not a slab.
                 It used to be a full-width filled button pinned to the bottom of the sidebar reading
                 "< Back to Toki" - the heaviest object in the window, for the action a user takes
                 least often, occupying the corner the eye rests on last. The reference puts exactly
                 this control in exactly this place as a small tinted square with a bare chevron, and
                 the label is unnecessary: there is one place back can go. -->
            <Button x:Name="BackBtn" Click="OnBack" Cursor="Hand" Focusable="False"
                    WindowChrome.IsHitTestVisibleInChrome="True"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="14,0,0,0"
                    Width="38" Height="30" ToolTip="Back to Toki">
                <Button.Template>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Bd" CornerRadius="10" Background="{DynamicResource Chip}">
                            <Path x:Name="Gl" Width="7" Height="12" Stretch="Uniform"
                                  HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                  Data="M 7,0 L 1,6 L 7,12" StrokeThickness="1.6"
                                  StrokeStartLineCap="Round" StrokeEndLineCap="Round" StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                                  Stroke="{DynamicResource Text}"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <!-- A STROKED PATH, not a font glyph. The chevron in the reference is a
                                 drawn stroke with round caps; the Fluent chevron character is a
                                 filled outline with flat ends and reads blunt beside it. Same
                                 finding as the play triangle (2026-09-26). -->
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ChipOnCard}"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Bd" Property="Opacity" Value="0.7"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Button.Template>
            </Button>

            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Top">
                <Button x:Name="MinBtn" Style="{StaticResource CaptionButton}" Content="&#xE921;"
                        Click="OnMinimise" ToolTip="Minimise"/>
                <!-- Glyph and tooltip both swap with the window state (E922 maximise / E923 restore),
                     in OnStateChanged - a maximise button that still says maximise is the classic
                     hand-rolled-chrome bug. -->
                <Button x:Name="MaxBtn" Style="{StaticResource CaptionButton}" Content="&#xE922;"
                        Click="OnMaximiseRestore" ToolTip="Maximise"/>
                <Button x:Name="CloseBtn" Style="{StaticResource CaptionButton}" Content="&#xE8BB;"
                        Click="OnCloseWindow" ToolTip="Close"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </Grid>

        <Grid Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Margin="9,0,0,9">
            <Border x:Name="SidebarKeyShadow" CornerRadius="18" Background="#01000000" IsHitTestVisible="False">
                <Border.Effect>
                    <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="22" ShadowDepth="6" Direction="270" Opacity="0.22" Color="#000000"/>
                </Border.Effect>
            </Border>
            <Border x:Name="SidebarAmbientShadow" CornerRadius="18" Background="#01000000" IsHitTestVisible="False">
                <Border.Effect>
                    <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="8" ShadowDepth="2" Direction="270" Opacity="0.12" Color="#000000"/>
                </Border.Effect>
            </Border>
            <Border x:Name="SidebarCard" CornerRadius="18" BorderThickness="1"
                    Background="{DynamicResource CardPlateOpaque}" BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CardRim}">
            <Grid>
            <!-- Concentric with the card: 18 minus the 1px rim it sits inside. -->
            <theme:CardMaterial Radius="17"/>
            <DockPanel Margin="12,24,12,16">
                <!-- The LOGO ROW: the WORDMARK ALONE.
                     It used to be the blue cloud plus "Toki", and it was a button so you could poke
                     him. Two problems, both the owner's (2026-08-14, with a screenshot): hovering it
                     drew a grey rounded box behind the pair, and there is no reason for a second Toki
                     to live here at all. "One Toki per surface" is a standing rule in CLAUDE.md, and
                     the Ask page already has the real one, big and pokeable, in the middle of the
                     page. This is a masthead - it identifies the app, it is not a control.
                     So: no mascot, no button, no hover. Just the name. -->
                <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="Toki" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}"
                           FontSize="20" FontWeight="SemiBold" Foreground="{DynamicResource TextHi}"
                           Margin="12,0,0,34" Height="28" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>

                <!-- BACK MOVED TO THE TITLE ROW (2026-09-27). It was a full-width filled pill docked
                     here at the bottom, and it was the heaviest object in the window for the action
                     taken least often. The sidebar now ends with the nav and nothing else, which is
                     what makes the empty space below it read as deliberate rather than unfinished. -->

                <!-- Nav items (built in code so the active one highlights) -->
                <StackPanel x:Name="Nav" DockPanel.Dock="Top"/>
            </DockPanel>
            </Grid>
            </Border>
        </Grid>

        <!-- ═══════════ Main pane ═══════════ -->
        <!-- No left seam any more: the sidebar floats as its own card with air around it, so a rule
             here would draw a line down the middle of that gap. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" Background="Transparent">
            <Grid x:Name="MainHost">

                <!-- ── Ask (conversation + landing) ── -->
                <Grid x:Name="AskPage">
                    <!-- The redesign's ambient depth (figma 348:5438): a cool teal-blue bloom rising
                         from the bottom of the page - the one page with atmosphere, because it is the
                         one page that is a conversation, not a document. Purely decorative.

                         It fades out at BOTH SIDES as well as upward. It used to be a plain full-width
                         rectangle, and a rectangle of tint has two hard edges: a vertical line down the
                         gap beside the sidebar card, and a square corner where it met the window's
                         rounded one (owner, 2026-08-14, with a before/after pair - "only have the
                         curved corner"). Atmosphere with a straight edge is not atmosphere, it is a
                         panel. The opacity mask costs one static brush and no per-frame work. -->
                    <!-- The stops live in ThemeManager's Gradients table now, as AskBloom. They were
                         written here as three DARK teal stops with no light counterpart, so a wash
                         drawn for a near-black page was painted over a near-white one and read as a
                         stain rather than as depth. -->
                    <Border IsHitTestVisible="False" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Height="420"
                            Background="{DynamicResource AskBloom}">
                        <Border.OpacityMask>
                            <LinearGradientBrush StartPoint="0,0.5" EndPoint="1,0.5">
                                <GradientStop Color="#00000000" Offset="0.00"/>
                                <GradientStop Color="#FF000000" Offset="0.14"/>
                                <GradientStop Color="#FF000000" Offset="0.86"/>
                                <GradientStop Color="#00000000" Offset="1.00"/>
                            </LinearGradientBrush>
                        </Border.OpacityMask>
                    </Border>
                    <DockPanel>
                        <!-- Top bar: current chat title, History (past conversations), and New chat. -->
                        <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Top" Margin="40,16,40,0">
                            <TextBlock x:Name="ChatTitle" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" Text="Ask Toki"/>
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
                                <Button x:Name="HistoryBtn" Style="{StaticResource PillButton}" Background="{DynamicResource Chip}"
                                        Padding="13,7" FontSize="13.5" Click="OnHistory">
                                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                                        <TextBlock Text="&#xE81C;" FontFamily="{StaticResource IconFont}" FontSize="13.5"
                                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,7,0"/>
                                        <TextBlock Text="History" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                    </StackPanel>
                                </Button>
                                <!-- New Chat is the WHITE pill (figma): the one primary action on this bar. -->
                                <Button x:Name="NewChatBtn" Style="{StaticResource WhiteButton}"
                                        Padding="13,7" FontSize="13.5" Margin="8,0,0,0" Click="OnNewChat" Content="+  New chat"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- Input bar - always docked at the bottom. -->
                        <!-- The redesign's prompt bar: a full capsule (r26) with the mic and a ROUND
                             send button seated at its right edge - one soft pill, not a box with icons. -->
                        <!-- The capsule is a RAISED surface, so it gets the card material: the plate
                             gradient and the top-bright rim, over the three-ring lift the design uses
                             under every floating capsule (GlassPill's idiom). Rings, not an Effect -
                             this box is full of live, editable text, and an Effect would push all of
                             it through an intermediate surface and cost ClearType on every keystroke. -->
                        <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" MaxWidth="720"
                              Margin="40,10,40,28">
                            <Rectangle RadiusX="26" RadiusY="26" Margin="-3,2,-3,-4" Fill="{DynamicResource Shadow1}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                            <Rectangle RadiusX="26" RadiusY="26" Margin="-2,2,-2,-3" Fill="{DynamicResource Shadow2}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                            <Rectangle RadiusX="26" RadiusY="26" Margin="-1,2,-1,-2" Fill="{DynamicResource Shadow3}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                        <Border x:Name="AskCapsule" Background="{DynamicResource CardPlateOpaque}" CornerRadius="26"
                                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CardRim}" BorderThickness="1" Padding="10,6,7,6" Cursor="IBeam">
                            <Grid>
                                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <TextBox x:Name="AskBox" Background="Transparent" BorderThickness="0"
                                         Foreground="{DynamicResource Text}" CaretBrush="{DynamicResource Text}"
                                         FontSize="15" VerticalContentAlignment="Center" Padding="12,11"
                                         FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}" KeyDown="OnAskKeyDown"/>
                                <!-- Voice overlay: replaces the box while listening / transcribing (Google-/Safari-
                                     style). A pulsing dot + status word; the mic drives it in code. -->
                                <StackPanel x:Name="VoiceStatus" Grid.Column="0" Orientation="Horizontal"
                                            VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="14,0,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed"
                                            IsHitTestVisible="False">
                                    <Ellipse x:Name="VoiceDot" Width="9" Height="9" Fill="{DynamicResource Coral}"
                                             VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
                                    <TextBlock x:Name="VoiceStatusText" Text="Listening…" FontSize="15"
                                               Foreground="{DynamicResource TextDim}" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}"
                                               VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                </StackPanel>
                                <Button Grid.Column="1" x:Name="MicBtn" Style="{StaticResource IconButton}"
                                        Content="&#xE720;" ToolTip="Ask by voice" Click="OnMic" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                <!-- The circular send: a filled disc so the bar ends in one clear action.
                                     It WAS a Chip-filled Border with an IconButton inside it, which put
                                     an r10 hover highlight inside an r18 circle - the box lit up in a
                                     different shape from the button it belongs to. RoundPuck IS the
                                     disc, so the hit target, the fill and the highlight are one shape.
                                     GLASS ON GLASS: it rests on the capsule, so its fill is a step of
                                     light above it (GlassFill) rather than the capsule's tint again.
                                     38, not 36: the capsule is r26 with a 7px right padding, so the
                                     disc seated in it is r19 - concentric, inner = outer minus pad. -->
                                <Button Grid.Column="2" x:Name="AskBtn" Style="{StaticResource RoundPuck}"
                                        Background="{DynamicResource GlassFill}" Foreground="{DynamicResource TextHi}"
                                        FontFamily="{StaticResource IconFont}" FontSize="15"
                                        Width="38" Height="38" Margin="4,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                        ToolTip="Ask" Click="OnAsk">
                                    <!-- The plane glyph carries its mass at the tail, so box-centred it reads
                                         as sitting left of the disc's middle. The correction is a RENDER
                                         transform, not a margin: a 1px left margin inside a centre-aligned
                                         ContentPresenter widens the content box by 1 and re-centres it, which
                                         moves the glyph half a pixel and then layout rounding eats that. This
                                         moves exactly one pixel and touches no layout. -->
                                    <TextBlock Text="&#xE724;">
                                        <TextBlock.RenderTransform><TranslateTransform X="1"/></TextBlock.RenderTransform>
                                    </TextBlock>
                                </Button>
                            </Grid>
                        </Border>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- Conversation scroll: the landing (greeting + suggestions) when empty; the Q&A thread once asked.
                             A floating "↓ Latest" pill (same as the notch AskView's JumpPill) appears when you've scrolled
                             UP away from the newest message - click = smooth-scroll to the end + re-anchor. -->
                        <Grid>
                            <ScrollViewer x:Name="ChatScroll" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" controls:SmoothScroll.Enabled="True">
                                <Grid>
                                    <!-- Ask landing - the welcome (Luma-style: a confident hero, one identity
                                         line, chips that invite action; soft pink depth from the mascot). -->
                                    <StackPanel x:Name="Landing" VerticalAlignment="Center" HorizontalAlignment="Center" MaxWidth="620" Margin="40,20">
                                        <!-- Hero: the mascot on a soft radial pink glow - subtle depth, not decoration. -->
                                        <Grid x:Name="LandingHero" HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,0,14">
                                            <!-- Soft BLUE glow (the mascot went blue with the redesign; the pink
                                                 halo read as a leftover of the old character). -->
                                            <Ellipse Width="240" Height="150" IsHitTestVisible="False">
                                                <Ellipse.Fill>
                                                    <RadialGradientBrush>
                                                        <GradientStop Color="#333D9BEA" Offset="0"/>
                                                        <GradientStop Color="#003D9BEA" Offset="1"/>
                                                    </RadialGradientBrush>
                                                </Ellipse.Fill>
                                            </Ellipse>
                                            <!-- A BUTTON, but a BARE one. It has to be a Button so that
                                                 poking Toki presses (it was a Border with a mouse
                                                 handler, which gives no feedback at all) - and it has
                                                 to be bare because Toki is a drawn character, not a
                                                 control: a grey rounded rectangle appearing behind him
                                                 on hover draws a box around a shape that is
                                                 deliberately not a box. BareButton keeps the compress
                                                 and drops the fill. -->
                                            <Button Style="{StaticResource BareButton}"
                                                    Padding="6" ToolTip="Poke Toki" Click="OnLandingMascotClick">
                                                <Viewbox Width="88" Height="78">
                                                    <controls:TokiBlob x:Name="LandingToki"/>
                                                </Viewbox>
                                            </Button>
                                        </Grid>
                                        <!-- DEMOTED from 30px (taste audit 2026-08-24). Squint at this page
                                             and the greeting was the only thing that survived, while the
                                             chips below it - the reason the page exists - read as
                                             secondary. A greeting carries no information; it should not
                                             be the largest type in the window. Demote, never promote. -->
                                        <TextBlock x:Name="Greeting" Style="{StaticResource Body}" FontSize="15" FontWeight="Medium"
                                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource TextSoft}"
                                                   TextAlignment="Center" TextWrapping="Wrap"/>
                                        <!-- AskHint no longer carries an identity line ("Your meeting
                                             assistant."): a sentence describing the interface to somebody
                                             already using it is narration. It still shows voice hints. -->
                                        <TextBlock x:Name="AskHint" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5" TextAlignment="Center"
                                                   Margin="0,6,0,22" TextWrapping="Wrap"/>
                                        <!-- A uniform 2-col grid, not a ragged wrap: equal-width chips
                                             read as one considered set, not offcuts. -->
                                        <UniformGrid x:Name="Suggestions" Columns="2" HorizontalAlignment="Center"/>
                                    </StackPanel>
                                    <StackPanel x:Name="Messages" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" MaxWidth="720"
                                                Margin="40,28,40,20" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
                                </Grid>
                            </ScrollViewer>
                            <!-- A BUTTON. It was a Border with a mouse handler: pill-shaped, hand
                                 cursor, sitting there asking to be clicked, and giving nothing back
                                 when you pressed it - no compress, no hover fill, no keyboard. -->
                            <Button x:Name="ChatJumpPill" Visibility="Collapsed" Opacity="0"
                                    Style="{StaticResource PillButton}" Background="{DynamicResource Chip}"
                                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Margin="0,0,0,8"
                                    Padding="11,5" Click="OnChatJumpToLatest">
                                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                                    <TextBlock Text="&#xE70D;" FontFamily="{StaticResource IconFont}" FontSize="12"
                                               Foreground="{DynamicResource Pink}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,6,0"/>
                                    <TextBlock Text="Latest" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}" FontSize="12" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                               Foreground="{DynamicResource TextSoft}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                </StackPanel>
                            </Button>
                        </Grid>
                    </DockPanel>
                </Grid>

                <!-- ── Library (list + detail) ── -->
                <Grid x:Name="LibraryPage" Visibility="Collapsed">
                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <!-- Fixed note-list column - no drag-resizer (owner: remove the VS Code resizer). -->
                        <ColumnDefinition x:Name="ListCol" Width="368"/>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                    <!-- NOT an inset Surface box: the list sits on the same background as the note pane and
                         fills its column edge-to-edge (like the Ask panel). No outer margin/box = no strip
                         of background showing between the box and the divider. Only the children carry a
                         small inner inset so text isn't flush to the edges. -->
                    <DockPanel x:Name="ListPanel" Grid.Column="0" Margin="20,18,16,0">
                        <!-- Title row: "Library" + count on the left; small New-note (+) and book "My notes"
                             filter on the right, vertically centred on the title. -->
                        <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Top" Margin="0,0,0,14">
                            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <!-- ONE LINE, as the frame draws it: "Library   48". It was a stacked title
                                 with a caption under it, which cost a second line for facts that fit
                                 beside the word. The scope label and its 1px divider appear ONLY for a
                                 filter that is actually on ("my notes"); "all on this PC" was a
                                 permanent label restating the product's whole premise. -->
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <TextBlock Text="Library" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}" FontSize="20"
                                           FontWeight="SemiBold" Foreground="{DynamicResource TextHi}"
                                           VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                <TextBlock x:Name="CountLabel" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}" FontSize="15"
                                           Foreground="{DynamicResource TextMid}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                           Margin="12,1,0,0"/>
                                <Border x:Name="CountRule" Width="1" Height="15" Margin="10,1,10,0"
                                        Background="{DynamicResource GlassBorder}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                <TextBlock x:Name="ScopeLabel" Visibility="Collapsed" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}"
                                           FontSize="15" Foreground="{DynamicResource TextMid}"
                                           VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,1,0,0"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <Button x:Name="MyNotesToggle" Style="{StaticResource IconButton}" Content="&#xE736;"
                                        Click="OnToggleMyNotes"/>
                                <Button x:Name="NewNoteBtn" Style="{StaticResource IconButton}" Content="&#xE710;"
                                        Margin="2,0,0,0" ToolTip="New note" Click="OnNewNote"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Grid>

                        <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Top" Margin="0,0,0,10">
                            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <!-- The frame's search field: 44 tall, radius 24, the same glass and shadow as
                                 every other surface, with a magnifier and a placeholder that says what
                                 the box accepts. It had none of those - a bare r10 rectangle with no
                                 icon and no placeholder, which is a box you have to guess at. -->
                            <!-- Same material as every other raised surface (plate + top-bright rim),
                                 lifted by the three-ring shadow rather than by an Effect: the box
                                 holds a live TextBox, and an Effect would rasterise the text you are
                                 typing through an intermediate surface on every keystroke.
                                 r22, not r24: at 44 tall a full capsule IS 22, and asking for 24 just
                                 gets silently clamped - the number should say what it draws. -->
                            <Grid Height="44">
                            <Rectangle RadiusX="22" RadiusY="22" Margin="-3,2,-3,-4" Fill="{DynamicResource Shadow1}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                            <Rectangle RadiusX="22" RadiusY="22" Margin="-2,2,-2,-3" Fill="{DynamicResource Shadow2}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                            <Rectangle RadiusX="22" RadiusY="22" Margin="-1,2,-1,-2" Fill="{DynamicResource Shadow3}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                            <Border x:Name="SearchCapsule" Background="{DynamicResource CardPlateOpaque}" CornerRadius="22"
                                    Padding="8,0,14,0" Cursor="IBeam"
                                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CardRim}" BorderThickness="1">
                                <Grid>
                                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                    <TextBlock Text="&#xE721;" FontFamily="{StaticResource IconFont}" FontSize="15"
                                               Foreground="{DynamicResource TextMid}" Opacity="0.75"
                                               VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="8,0,8,0" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                                    <Grid Grid.Column="1">
                                        <!-- "Search these notes - you: / them" taught the query
                                             syntax in the placeholder, where it was clipped by the
                                             field's own width and read as an error message. One
                                             word. The you:/them: filters still work and belong in
                                             help, not on the resting state of an input. -->
                                        <TextBlock x:Name="SearchPlaceholder" Text="Search"
                                                   IsHitTestVisible="False" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                   FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}" FontSize="15"
                                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource TextLow}"/>
                                        <TextBox x:Name="SearchBox" Background="Transparent" BorderThickness="0"
                                                 Foreground="{DynamicResource TextHi}" CaretBrush="{DynamicResource TextHi}"
                                                 VerticalContentAlignment="Center" FontSize="15" TextChanged="OnSearch"/>
                                    </Grid>
                                </Grid>
                            </Border>
                            </Grid>
                            <!-- Sort/filter menu: Newest/Oldest/Longest/Shortest/Open tasks. A 44x44
                                 glass button in the frame, matching the field beside it, not a bare glyph.
                                 MATCHING means the same material: the rings and the rim are drawn around
                                 it here rather than in PillButton, because that style is shared by every
                                 pill in the app and most of them are not raised surfaces. The rim sits
                                 ABOVE the button so it traces the same box the hover fills. -->
                            <Grid Grid.Column="1" Width="44" Height="44" Margin="12,0,0,0">
                                <Rectangle RadiusX="13" RadiusY="13" Margin="-3,2,-3,-4" Fill="{DynamicResource Shadow1}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                                <Rectangle RadiusX="13" RadiusY="13" Margin="-2,2,-2,-3" Fill="{DynamicResource Shadow2}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                                <Rectangle RadiusX="13" RadiusY="13" Margin="-1,2,-1,-2" Fill="{DynamicResource Shadow3}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                                <Button x:Name="SortBtn" Style="{StaticResource PillButton}"
                                        Background="{DynamicResource CardPlateOpaque}"
                                        Foreground="{DynamicResource TextMid}"
                                        Width="44" Height="44" Padding="0"
                                        FontFamily="{StaticResource IconFont}" FontSize="15"
                                        Content="&#xE8CB;" ToolTip="Sort &amp; filter" Click="OnSortMenu"/>
                                <Border x:Name="SortRim" CornerRadius="13" BorderThickness="1"
                                        BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CardRim}" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                            </Grid>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- The meeting list only builds the rows that are on screen. The ScrollViewer is
                             INSIDE the control's template on purpose: an ItemsControl wrapped in an outer
                             ScrollViewer is measured with infinite height, so every row is realised and
                             virtualization does nothing. ScrollUnit=Pixel keeps SmoothScroll's per-pixel
                             glide (item-unit scrolling would jump a whole card per notch).
                             The empty state is an overlay, not a row, so it can't be virtualized away. -->
                        <Grid>
                            <views:MeetingList x:Name="Cards"
                                               VirtualizingPanel.IsVirtualizing="True"
                                               VirtualizingPanel.VirtualizationMode="Recycling"
                                               VirtualizingPanel.ScrollUnit="Pixel">
                                <ItemsControl.Template>
                                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="ItemsControl">
                                        <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" CanContentScroll="True"
                                                      Focusable="False" controls:SmoothScroll.Enabled="True">
                                            <ItemsPresenter/>
                                        </ScrollViewer>
                                    </ControlTemplate>
                                </ItemsControl.Template>
                                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                        <VirtualizingStackPanel/>
                                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                            </views:MeetingList>
                            <TextBlock x:Name="CardsEmpty" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5"
                                       TextAlignment="Center" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,24,0,0"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Top" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
                        </Grid>
                    </DockPanel>

                    <!-- One subtle hairline divides the list from the note (the splitter that used to draw
                         the seam is gone). -->
                    <Border Grid.Column="1" Background="{DynamicResource Ink}"
                            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1,0,0,0">
                        <!-- ONE scroll fills the whole pane: the note (toolbar → title → body) is a single
                             RichTextBox, so its scrollbar runs the FULL height from the very top of the pane,
                             like a normal app scroll - not starting below a pinned toolbar. Left/right padding
                             lives inside the RichTextBox so the scrollbar sits flush at the pane's right edge. -->
                        <ContentControl x:Name="DetailBody"
                                        HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch" VerticalContentAlignment="Stretch"/>
                    </Border>
                </Grid>

                <!-- ── Templates ── -->
                <!-- Left-aligned with the SAME padding as Settings: every page in this window starts at
                     the same left edge, so switching tabs doesn't shift the text under you. (Templates
                     was the one centred page.) -->
                <ScrollViewer x:Name="TemplatesPage" Visibility="Collapsed" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                              Padding="40,34,40,34" controls:SmoothScroll.Enabled="True">
                    <StackPanel x:Name="TemplatesList" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"/>
                </ScrollViewer>

                <!-- ── Settings - THE home for preferences: name · appearance · AI summaries · calendar
                        · about. Dividers-not-boxes: hairlines separate the sections. ── -->
                <ScrollViewer x:Name="SettingsPage" Visibility="Collapsed" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                              Padding="40,34,40,34" controls:SmoothScroll.Enabled="True">
                    <!-- The redesign's settings page (figma 353:9154): ONE centred column, each section a
                         big title and a rounded CARD of rows. NO explainer under a title and no caption
                         under a row (owner, 2026-08-20: "there's a lot of text, keep it minimal and
                         clean"). A page that narrates each of its own controls reads as generated copy.
                         Where a label needs help, rename the label. -->
                    <StackPanel MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Center">
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,0,0,26">
                            <TextBlock Text="Settings" Style="{StaticResource PageTitle}"/>
                            <!-- Bottom-aligned with a nudge - WPF has no Baseline alignment for siblings. -->
                            <TextBlock Text="Toki 1.0" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Margin="14,0,0,4"/>
                        </StackPanel>

                        <TextBlock Text="What should Toki call you?" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}"/>
                        <Border x:Name="NameCard" Background="{DynamicResource SurfaceHi}" CornerRadius="14" Padding="14,0"
                                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1"
                                HorizontalAlignment="Stretch">
                            <TextBox x:Name="LibNameBox" Background="Transparent" BorderThickness="0"
                                     Foreground="{DynamicResource Text}" CaretBrush="{DynamicResource Text}"
                                     Height="46" FontSize="15" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}"
                                     VerticalContentAlignment="Center" MaxLength="40"
                                     TextChanged="OnLibNameChanged" KeyDown="OnLibNameKey"/>
                        </Border>

                        <!-- Confirm / cancel only appear once there's an unsaved change. -->
                        <StackPanel x:Name="LibNameActions" Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,14,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed">
                            <Button x:Name="LibNameSave" Style="{StaticResource WhiteButton}" Content="Save"
                                    Padding="26,9" Click="OnLibNameSave"/>
                            <Button x:Name="LibNameCancel" Style="{StaticResource PillButton}" Background="Transparent"
                                    Content="Cancel" Padding="20,9" Margin="8,0,0,0" Click="OnLibNameCancel"/>
                        </StackPanel>

                        <!-- Positive reinforcement after a successful save. -->
                        <StackPanel x:Name="LibNameSaved" Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,14,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed">
                            <TextBlock Text="&#xE73E;" FontFamily="{StaticResource IconFont}" FontSize="13.5"
                                       Foreground="{DynamicResource Green}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,8,0"/>
                            <TextBlock x:Name="LibNameSavedText" Style="{StaticResource Body}" FontSize="13.5"
                                       Foreground="{DynamicResource Green}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </StackPanel>

                        <!-- ── Appearance: the redesign's CARD of rows - one rounded surface, rows split
                             by hairlines, the switch on the right. Directly under the name, as the frame
                             orders it (353:9154). ── -->
                        <!-- Divider removed 2026-09-27: 28px of air on each side already separates these
                             sections. Material 3 is explicit that a divider belongs only where elements
                             "cannot be separated using white space", and that too many lines make an
                             interface look cluttered. The gap does the work; the line was noise. -->
                        <Border Height="0" Margin="0,28,0,0"/>
                        <!-- NO CAPTION under this heading (taste audit 2026-08-24). It read "How Toki
                             looks and what it shows in a share." - the first half restates the word
                             "Appearance" to somebody who just read it, which is the interface narrating
                             itself. The second half was real information, and it has moved to the row it
                             actually describes ("Show Toki in screen recordings"), where it can be
                             acted on. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Appearance" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}" Margin="0,0,0,12"/>
                        <Border x:Name="AppearanceCard" Background="{DynamicResource SurfaceHi}" CornerRadius="16"
                                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1">
                            <StackPanel>
                                <Grid Margin="16,13">
                                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                    <TextBlock Text="Dark theme" Style="{StaticResource BodyStrong}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                    <CheckBox x:Name="LibThemeToggle" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource TokiSwitch}"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" Click="OnLibThemeToggle"/>
                                </Grid>
                                <Border Height="1" Background="{DynamicResource Hairline}" Margin="16,0"/>
                                <Grid Margin="16,13">
                                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                    <StackPanel VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,16,0">
                                        <TextBlock Text="Glassy notch" Style="{StaticResource BodyStrong}"/>
                                    </StackPanel>
                                    <CheckBox x:Name="LibGlassToggle" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource TokiSwitch}"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" Click="OnLibGlassToggle"/>
                                </Grid>
                                <Border Height="1" Background="{DynamicResource Hairline}" Margin="16,0"/>
                                <Grid Margin="16,13">
                                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                    <StackPanel VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,16,0">
                                        <TextBlock Text="Show Toki in screen recordings" Style="{StaticResource BodyStrong}"/>
                                        <!-- The one real fact from the deleted "Appearance" caption, on the
                                             row it describes: a CONSEQUENCE, which is what helper text is
                                             for. Off means Toki is invisible to anything that captures the
                                             screen, which is not guessable from the label alone. -->
                                        <TextBlock Text="Off keeps Toki out of screen shares and recordings."
                                                   Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="12" Margin="0,2,0,0"
                                                   TextWrapping="Wrap"/>
                                    </StackPanel>
                                    <CheckBox x:Name="LibGhostToggle" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource TokiSwitch}"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" Click="OnLibGhostToggle"/>
                                </Grid>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Border>

                        <!-- ── Names Toki should always get right ──
                             The roster behind speaker naming and name correction. AppSettings.PinnedNames
                             has been read by NameLexicon since T2, but nothing could ever write it - the
                             escape hatch existed with no handle on it. A name typed here is TRUSTED: it
                             beats what the recogniser heard, it lets a spoken name be attached to a voice,
                             and it is the only fix for a name the library cannot settle by frequency. -->
                        <!-- Divider removed 2026-09-27: 28px of air on each side already separates these
                             sections. Material 3 is explicit that a divider belongs only where elements
                             "cannot be separated using white space", and that too many lines make an
                             interface look cluttered. The gap does the work; the line was noise. -->
                        <Border Height="0" Margin="0,28,0,0"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="People Toki should spell right" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="One per line, or comma separated." Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5" Margin="0,-8,0,12"/>
                        <Border x:Name="PinnedCard" Background="{DynamicResource SurfaceHi}" CornerRadius="12" Padding="14,10"
                                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1"
                                HorizontalAlignment="Stretch">
                            <TextBox x:Name="LibPinnedBox" Background="Transparent" BorderThickness="0"
                                     Foreground="{DynamicResource Text}" CaretBrush="{DynamicResource Text}"
                                     MinHeight="72" MaxHeight="150" FontSize="15" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}"
                                     AcceptsReturn="True" TextWrapping="Wrap" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                                     TextChanged="OnLibPinnedChanged"/>
                        </Border>

                        <StackPanel x:Name="LibPinnedActions" Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,14,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed">
                            <Button x:Name="LibPinnedSave" Style="{StaticResource WhiteButton}" Content="Save"
                                    Padding="26,9" Click="OnLibPinnedSave"/>
                            <Button Style="{StaticResource PillButton}" Background="Transparent"
                                    Content="Cancel" Padding="20,9" Margin="8,0,0,0" Click="OnLibPinnedCancel"/>
                        </StackPanel>
                        <StackPanel x:Name="LibPinnedSaved" Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,14,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed">
                            <TextBlock Text="&#xE73E;" FontFamily="{StaticResource IconFont}" FontSize="13.5"
                                       Foreground="{DynamicResource Green}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,8,0"/>
                            <TextBlock x:Name="LibPinnedSavedText" Style="{StaticResource Body}" FontSize="13.5"
                                       Foreground="{DynamicResource Green}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </StackPanel>

                        <!-- ── Re-clean saved transcripts ──
                             OFFERED, never automatic. Every improvement to turn-merging or name
                             correction makes older transcripts look worse, and the tempting move is to
                             fix them quietly at startup. A transcript is a record of what people said;
                             rewriting one the user has already read is not a favour. So: a button, a
                             plain statement of what it will and will not touch, and a count afterwards. -->
                        <!-- Divider removed 2026-09-27: 28px of air on each side already separates these
                             sections. Material 3 is explicit that a divider belongs only where elements
                             "cannot be separated using white space", and that too many lines make an
                             interface look cluttered. The gap does the work; the line was noise. -->
                        <Border Height="0" Margin="0,28,0,0"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="Re-clean saved transcripts" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="Tidies older transcripts. Your notes and ticks stay." Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5" Margin="0,-8,0,12"/>
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                            <Button x:Name="LibRecleanButton" Style="{StaticResource PillButton}"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource Chip}" Content="Re-clean transcripts"
                                    Padding="18,9" Click="OnLibReclean"/>
                            <TextBlock x:Name="LibRecleanStatus" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="14,0,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
                        </StackPanel>

                        <!-- ── AI summaries (the mode + BYO-API-key flow, moved whole from the notch) ── -->
                        <!-- Divider removed 2026-09-27: 28px of air on each side already separates these
                             sections. Material 3 is explicit that a divider belongs only where elements
                             "cannot be separated using white space", and that too many lines make an
                             interface look cluttered. The gap does the work; the line was noise. -->
                        <Border Height="0" Margin="0,28,0,0"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="AI summaries" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="On-device by default, or bring your own key." Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5" Margin="0,-8,0,12"/>
                        <views:AiSummariesSection x:Name="AiSection"/>

                        <!-- ── Calendars ──
                             TWO independent connections, not a choice between them. A work Outlook
                             calendar and a personal Google one is an ordinary combination, and a
                             notetaker watching only one of them misses half the meetings without ever
                             saying so. Connect either, or both; the agenda is merged.

                             A CONNECTED row shrinks to a small provider mark plus the account it is
                             signed in as (owner's ask): once it is done, it is a fact to confirm at a
                             glance, not an action taking up a button's worth of space. -->
                        <!-- Divider removed 2026-09-27: 28px of air on each side already separates these
                             sections. Material 3 is explicit that a divider belongs only where elements
                             "cannot be separated using white space", and that too many lines make an
                             interface look cluttered. The gap does the work; the line was noise. -->
                        <Border Height="0" Margin="0,28,0,0"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="Calendars" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="Connect one or both. Toki merges them into one agenda." Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5" Margin="0,-8,0,12"/>

                        <!-- Google -->
                        <Grid x:Name="LibGoogleRow">
                            <!-- Three columns, not a horizontal stack: a StackPanel measures its
                                 children with INFINITE width, so a wrapping status line simply ran
                                 underneath the button instead of wrapping before it. -->
                            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <Border x:Name="LibGoogleMark" Width="26" Height="26" CornerRadius="13"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource Chip}" Margin="0,0,10,0"
                                    VerticalAlignment="Center" Visibility="Collapsed">
                                <TextBlock Text="G" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}" FontSize="13.5"
                                           FontWeight="SemiBold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Text}"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                            </Border>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,16,0">
                                <TextBlock Text="Google Calendar" Style="{StaticResource BodyStrong}"/>
                                <TextBlock x:Name="LibGoogleStatus" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5"
                                           Margin="0,2,0,0" TextWrapping="Wrap"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <Button x:Name="LibGoogleConnectBtn" Style="{StaticResource PillButton}" Padding="20,9"
                                        Click="OnLibGoogleConnect"/>
                                <Button x:Name="LibGoogleDisconnectBtn" Style="{StaticResource PillButton}" Background="Transparent"
                                        Foreground="{DynamicResource Coral}" Content="Disconnect" Padding="16,9" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                                        ToolTip="Sign out of Google and remove the stored token" Click="OnLibGoogleDisconnect"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- Outlook -->
                        <Grid x:Name="LibOutlookRow" Margin="0,18,0,0">
                            <!-- Three columns, not a horizontal stack: a StackPanel measures its
                                 children with INFINITE width, so a wrapping status line simply ran
                                 underneath the button instead of wrapping before it. -->
                            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <Border x:Name="LibOutlookMark" Width="26" Height="26" CornerRadius="13"
                                    Background="{DynamicResource Chip}" Margin="0,0,10,0"
                                    VerticalAlignment="Center" Visibility="Collapsed">
                                <TextBlock Text="O" FontFamily="{StaticResource UiFont}" FontSize="13.5"
                                           FontWeight="SemiBold" Foreground="{DynamicResource Text}"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                            </Border>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,16,0">
                                <TextBlock Text="Outlook" Style="{StaticResource BodyStrong}"/>
                                <TextBlock x:Name="LibOutlookStatus" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" FontSize="13.5"
                                           Margin="0,2,0,0" TextWrapping="Wrap"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <Button x:Name="LibOutlookConnectBtn" Style="{StaticResource PillButton}" Padding="20,9"
                                        Click="OnLibOutlookConnect"/>
                                <Button x:Name="LibOutlookDisconnectBtn" Style="{StaticResource PillButton}" Background="Transparent"
                                        Foreground="{DynamicResource Coral}" Content="Disconnect" Padding="16,9" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                                        ToolTip="Remove the stored Outlook token from this PC" Click="OnLibOutlookDisconnect"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- ── About ── -->
                        <!-- Divider removed 2026-09-27: 28px of air on each side already separates these
                             sections. Material 3 is explicit that a divider belongs only where elements
                             "cannot be separated using white space", and that too many lines make an
                             interface look cluttered. The gap does the work; the line was noise. -->
                        <Border Height="0" Margin="0,28,0,0"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="About" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}"/>
                        <!-- A BUTTON, and its hover comes from the shared style rather than from two
                             hand-written MouseEnter/MouseLeave handlers that had to re-implement what
                             every other row in this window gets for free (and could not press). -->
                        <Button Margin="-8,8,-8,0" Padding="8,10" Style="{StaticResource RowButton}"
                                Background="Transparent" HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
                                Click="OnLibOpenSource">
                            <Grid>
                                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <StackPanel VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,10,0">
                                    <TextBlock Text="Built with - open source &amp; licences" Style="{StaticResource BodyStrong}"/>
                                </StackPanel>
                                <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="&#xE76C;" FontFamily="{StaticResource IconFont}"
                                           FontSize="13.5" Foreground="{DynamicResource TextDim}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                            </Grid>
                        </Button>

                    </StackPanel>
                </ScrollViewer>

            </Grid>
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</Window>
